the massive migration of wildebeests  across the Serengeti has a huge impact  on the ecosystem one overlooked aspect  has been what happens when the  wildebeest drowned during river crossing  every year  1.2 million wildebeest move across the  savanna consuming more than 4,500 tons  of grass every day in an 1800 kilometre  circuit between Kenya and Tanzania as  they follow the Rings their herding  instinct is strong because even though  being in a crowd leads to stiff  competition for food this part of Africa  has so many lions and other predators  that strength and numbers wins out even  if it means sometimes blindly swimming  towards death almost annually thousands  drown and river crossings often because  the opposite banks are too steep or too  many are trying to cross at one time for  the past six years researchers have  taken stock of the pile up of carcasses  surveyed the parade of scavengers  assisting in their decomposition and  used modeling and chemical analysis to  track where nutrients provided by these  dead animals wind up in the food chain  about 6500 animals drowned each year  some being the equivalent of 10 blue  whale carcasses into the river half of  that biomass is bone which takes seven  years to decay carcasses accumulate at  bends in the river an outcrop annually  they add about 13 tons of phosphorous 25  tons of nitrogen in the 107 tons of  carbon to the ecosystem the nutrients  come in pulses that last about a month  Beach and can temporarily double or even  quadruple some nutrient levels vultures  and storks flock to the site for the  feast and likely move wildebeest derive  nutrients in land after they fly away  crocodiles too are known to concrete  chemical analyses showed that up to 50%  of the diet of local fish comes from the  carcasses when they're present after the  meat disappears the remaining bones  begin to support a film of microbes that  in turn become food for fish and other  river dwellers on the positive side the  wildebeest drownings only involve about  0.7 percent of the total herd illegal  harvesting starvation and predation take  many more  [Music]  [Music]
 

